Date: Fri, 14 Jan 1994 11:22:07 -0800 (PST)
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: If Uploads Come First

John K Clark writes:

> old 8088. I think we could conservatively say the first
> primitive uploads would think 1,000,000 times as fast as we do.
> An upload mind would need great patience to communicate with us.

I'm quite skeptical about "uploads" in general, but _if_ they happen
at all, I'd bet just the opposite of what John says here: I predict
the "first primitive uploads" would think _much more slowly_ than we
do, not a million times faster. This is because I think the "first
primitive uploads" will certainly not be the replacement of
bio-neurons with artificial neurons, on a one-for-one basis as the
Moravec-popularized scenario has it.

Jumping from where we are today to a million-fold speedup is unlikely,
and the things we build in between will likely occupy our efforts for
many years to come.

Personally, while I'm skeptical about uploading, for engineering
reasons, and agree with Robin Hanson that AI is likely a tough nut to
crack (becuase minds are complicated, evolved objects with great
logical depth and few reductionistic rules), I'm quite enthused by the
prospects for what I like to call "synthetic intelligence."

Systems with 10,000 times the current compute capability, similar
increases in memory, a diverse knowledge base (a la Lenat's work, in
20 years), multiple paradigms (neural nets, connectionist, subsumption
architectures, rule-based, aptical foddering, etc.), all mixed
together with crude personality emulators, reflective reasoning,
and--above all--with evolutionary learning strategies, will likely
produce machines which are far more "interesting" in an alien way than
a mere uploaded human would likely be.

> Lets suppose that AI is a very difficult problem that would take
> 1000 years of intensive research to solve, for an upload that
> works out to about 8 hours.  

An interesting argument, though. That is, if Robin is right and
uploads come first, and they _do_ think faster--and presumably
tirelessly and constantly--then the "AI problem" gets solved _the next
day_ or so. (I'm still skeptical.)

This is the "AI runaway" scenario so beloved of SF writers, as in "The
Forbin Project" and a dozen other stories in which a machine designs
its successor which designs _its_ successor and so on. This is why the
"AI Singularity" used to get talked about almost as much as the
Nanotech Singularity now gets talked about.

--Tim May


