Vince Kerchner writes:

> 2.  Is the Extropy list an Extropian relationship?  I think the answer
> to this is a resounding "yes".  Recently, I have heard many of my
> Extropian friends comment on how they consider other members of the
> list, especially those in their own geographic areas, to be members of
> their extended family, often over and above actual members of their
> biological families.  It is interesting to note that most of the
> people I have heard express this feeling have been women.

Perhaps not surprisingly, I don't hold to this point of view.

Some Extropians list members I know personally I find to be very
interesting to talk to, to read the words of, and occasionally even to
attend parties with. Some I find less interesting. But none of them do
I view as part of an "extended family" except in the most banal sense.

Which is also why I resented deeply the overyly-familiar criticism of
me ("lizard eyes," "You scare the hell out of me," "You petrify me. I
hope this amuses you.") by some on this list. We are just a _mailing
list_ folks, not a communal, touchy-feely, encounter group! (Though I
do admit that the hot tub parties, even if not in Marin, are often the
most enjoyable and relaxing events.)

So leave me out of this increasingly cloying atmosphere, where those
of us, as but one example, who choose not to go communally shooting
are then accused of being "anal-retentive." (I'm not saying Vince, or
Geoff, has said this, of course. In fact, I suspect Vince and I are on
the same wavelength on this issue, but the increasing mood of an
"extended family" or family substitute--which is what I think it is,
as many list members are living alone or away from their true
familites--is leading to these "boundary problems," where folks make
inappropriate comments that show their boundaries are poorly defined.)

To me, the list is a bunch of people roughly interested in the same
kinds of things I'm interested in. People who are for the most part
very bright and who read a lot. This makes for interesting
discussions.

It's not an extended family, it may not even last (for me, at least),
I can see a time in the not too distant future when I'll have gotten
sufficiently tired of the 18th discussion of uploads, the 23rd
discussion of PPL, and the 47th go-round on cryptography that I just
can't take it anymore. At that point I'll leave, and that'll be that.
The "binding energy" is just too low, as it is in so many
relationships in these days of great freedom of choice. At least with
my real family (my biological chauvinism may be showing), the binding
energy ensures that simple disagreements and even disgust will not
result in severing ties.


Maybe it's just me. But then I've never fully grokked the significance
of the Extropian Principles (drum roll), and find the whole discussion
of what is and what is not an "Extropian relationship" to be
uninteresting.

Which is why I'll stop here.

--Tim May



