From: vincek@netcom.com (Vince Kerchner)
Subject: If uploads come first

I think John Clark makes a very good point concerning the use of
intermediate stages of augmented intelligence to solve the overall
AI/Uploading problem.  Although the "full" AI problem may be very
difficult (if not impossible) to solve given contemporary
understanding and "human" abilities, the use of an intermediate
cybernetic stage --whether we understand it or not-- could be
immensely advantageous, so much so that the "entire" problem could be
solved in essentially the same objective time as the intermediate
problem. 

I wonder if this idea has ramifications in other areas of AI,
especially in automated theorem proving, or even in the issue of
axiomatic set decidability itself.  Given that some sentences cannot
be reached from an initial axiom/production state, it may be possible
to go from such a state to an intermediate axiom/production state
which is different (but reachable) from the first state, and then use
that new state as a platform for completing the original proof.

This also reminds me of Feynman's approach to nanotech: building
smaller and smaller machines, each of which are used to build yet
smaller machines.  I realize that Drexler and Merkle have serious
objections to this approach, and have based their own on a "bottom-up"
paradigm.  But, given the technical difficulties and other unknowns
which seem to be inherent in this approach as well, it might be worth
reexamining the "top-down" or another intermediate approach. 

--Vince

