From: edgar@spectrx.saigon.com (Edgar W. Swank)
Subject: Children as Property

I'll put in my 2 cents worth here. Dani Elder said,

    The interesting question is whether one automatically obtains
    property ownership in one's self at birth.  I find this logically
    inconsistent.  Any other product of one's labor is considered ones
    property.  This includes products of one's body (breast milk and
    long hair are currently saleable products, and a spare kidney and
    blood could be sold also).  A child is just another product of the
    body, which the mother has invested much effort in creating, and
    which the parents then expend much further effort in raising -
    i.e.  converting from a useless raw material - a baby - into a
    useful end product - an adult.

    A baby at birth provided none of the effort to create itself - all
    the raw materials came from the mother (minus one sperm from the
    father), and the mother provided the support system - the womb,
    etc.  Even if you consider the baby to have put in the effort
    itself to take the raw materials and self-grow, I would claim that
    the parents at least have an interest in the life-property to the
    extent they contributed to it.

    In this view, you own your own life, but your parents stick you
    with a bill for child-rearing at the time you are old enough to
    contract.  Then you can pay them back over time, or go to a
    lender, borrow the money to pay off your parents, then pay the
    lender back over time.

Dani forgets that the child never agreed to any of this. He didn't ask
to be created.  The same principal as if someone sends you merchandise
you didn't order or agree to pay for in the mail. You are not
obligated to pay; you are not even obligated to return it.

The are two phases to consider.  In "infancy" the child is incapable
of communication beyond crying and smiling and is certainly incapable
of contract, so parents assume certain "rights" to make decisions for
the child.  It seems to me that whatever parental "rights" are, the
parents do own those and can free sell them or give them away. So
parents should be free to sell their children in the sense of selling
their parental rights to some other party (but not to sell the child
into slavery for example).

At some age, which probably varies with the individual child (you
parents help me here), but long before the legal age of adulthood,
children become capable of communicating reasonably complex ideas and
linking speech to action.  They become capable of understand the
concept of a promise of future action for some present reward or vice
versa. At this time, parental "rights" to make decisions for children
(should) disappear because they are capable of making their own
decisions (at least until proven otherwise).

At either stage, it seems to me, there are no -inherent- obligations
of parent & child to each other.

Infants are, pretty much by definition, incapable of fulfilling
obligations. Parents (should be) free to decide to abandon their
infant at any time, or to sell it (actually just their parental
rights).

After infancy, parents can insist that the child obey their rules
(which might include accepting a debt for previous services) or find
somewhere else to live.  Children are always free to leave home and/or
look for better substitute parents.  This might work better for the
child than you might expect. I understand there is a shortage of
adoptable children, at least here in the USA.

I think the above is closer to a "logically consistent system." It
allows neither parent or child to initiate force (or use the state to
initiate force) against the other.

