Subject: BOT: How do trees drink?

Michael Clive Price said in msg #94-1-1708:

    > How do trees transport water from their roots to their leaves?

    With a bit of help from my friends, here's a possible answer:
    Trees are able to lift water beyond 33 feet (= 1 atmosphere)
    because of their strong hydrogen bonds.  Pure water has a high
    tensile strength (=> high latent heat of evaporation).  Provided
    the sap is free from particles for bubbles to nucleate around
    (including the sides of the xylem tube) then high negative
                                                      ~~~~~~~~
    pressures (of perhaps thousands of atmospheres, according to my
    ~~~~~~~~~
    maths) can be withstood before the sap starts boiling.

    Thus trees may grow as tall (almost) as they like and still drink.

I was shocked to read Mike Price spouting this complete nonsense. Mike
may understand advanced physics but has apparently forgotten what he
should have learned in high school.  Or perhaps he is just playing
with us (Mike?).

Of course there is no such thing as "negative pressure".  Even Linda
Lovelace can't suck better than a perfect vacuum (complete absence of
gas molecules in some enclosed space -- zero pressure).  A vacuum
can't pull anything.  When it -appears- a vacuum is drawing up fluid
in a tube, it's really the -external atmospheric (positive) pressure-
that's exerting the force.

It really is impossible to suck water up more than about 33 ft. at sea
level.  Deep wells have pumps at the -bottom- to raise the water up.

I'm not a botanist, so I don't know the details of how trees work. But
capillary action in the very narrow xylem tubes could account for it.
Capillary action works through -adhesion-, the tendency of different
substances to stick together and does not depend on atmospheric
pressure.

