Subject: If uploads come first

[Well, today I have more time to read and post...]

>From: John K Clark <johnkc@well.sf.ca.us>

> I think we could conservatively say the first
> primitive uploads would think 1,000,000 times as fast as we do.

I have a radically different scenario:  The cost of building a machine
that can hold a mind that thinks 1,000,000 times normal versus one that
runs at ordinary speed should be considered.  It might be the case that
normal speeds acheived by time-sharing neurons fast would bring the cost
down by a huge factor, say, up to 1,000,000 times.  (Although, most (all?)
harware neurons today cannot change their weights quickly.)

I've done a scenario about slowing down a mind *and* simplifying it
in order to achieve Moravec's end result in a descaled way.  Bandwith
could be limited: consider that intelligence can arise with poor eyesight
(say, 10,000 less resolution in pixels and colorspace).  As a result, the 
processing power of a huge part of the brain needs 10,000 times less bandwith
[warning: linear assumptions].  For sound, cut off the sample rate at 8kHz 
(speech, but no music).

For a proof of concept, slow the machine down from real time to the 
degree needed...that could provide a 100 times cost reduction (more than
that makes the experiments more costly for other factor since the experiments
would take a long time).  A time slowdown doesn't suffer from any kind of 
linear assumptions.

Is that too expensive?  Okay, eliminate vision altogether.  That's at least
1/3 of the brain.

At one point, I came up with a figure of 400 workstations--that would
be sufficient to do this kind of human brain emulation (with primitive
vision).  

Next problem: neural architecture.  That's the real hard part, and I think
Robin is referring to this part as being so difficult that it might be
easier to copy a working system.   


