Date: Fri, 14 Jan 1994 17:23:15 -0800
From: vincek@netcom.com (Vince Kerchner)
Subject: Ammo warning

           !!!!!!!!!!!!! WARNING !!!!!!!!!!!

I just came back from the range (Sunnyvale) where I had a bad
experience with some 9mm ammo.  What happened was that on several
shells (3 before I stopped shooting) the primers would pop out of the
back when fired.  Not only did this throw off my aim, but it also
jammed the gun (I was using a Glock 17).  I recovered the empty brass,
and they, well, have little holes in the middle where the primers
should be.  I recovered one of the primers too, and it, well, looks like
an empty primer.  The jams happened because while the brass was
ejected, the primers fell back into the breech mechanism of the gun,
preventing it from closing.

The range master (Jim Sanchez) told me to stop shooting, as this could
damage the breech face of the gun, and to take the ammo back where I
got it (Eddy's).  This I will do tomorrow morning.  For those of you
who may have bought this ammo, it comes in a red box with a gold star
and the text "50 Centerfire Cartridges / 9mm Ammunition / Warning:
Keep out of reach of children."  It is made in China, and was sold for
about $10 for 50.  I have bought this ammo before, and had no problems
with it.  However, I'll stick to USA (Winchester) from now on.

FYI, I also shot about 75 rounds of USA .45 (in a Glock 21), and had 3
jams (all on loading).  This is typical for this gun/ammo combination.
I also put 13 Black Talons (Winchester) through the .45 with no
problems, nor have I had any problems with Black Talon in other
calibers/guns.  However, I don't shoot that much of the expensive
stuff, so my data is limited.

All of the above is why I don't recommend autos for anything but
target, and why I do recommend that you thoroughly check out your ammo,
clips, and guns before you depend on them.  Even my Sig-Sauer P226
jams on occasion, although that's the gun I depend on.

--Vince

